re your  task is a combination of an environment  which you want your robot to  be living in  and some sort of objective for what you  want your robot to do right and so  um if we have this sort of specification  then  robot design you can write down using  this very simple problem formulation  right so we want to maximize our  objective f over the space of possible  robots subject to physics right and so  this is how  we have tried to more formally think  about the problem of robot design of  course  i've hidden a lot of details in this  description  right  but i mean for different types of robot  descriptions or different types of task  descriptions you can get different types  of designs or different types of  algorithms that allow you to solve this  problem  so  when i was doing my phd at mit  we thought about well what if we want a  robot that's going to walk forward in a  flat ground and the robot is just  a  kinematic chain right  made of rigid bodies and  rotational joints  right so in that case what we can do  is we can build these systems that allow  you to compose different types of robot  geometries with different types of  kinematics  and to simulate how they  are able to locomote forward in a flat  on a flat plane  and  if all of the  you know geometries are resolved inside  of the software the software make sure  that the controller makes sense  that all that the robot can be  fabricated then we can take this design  and we can convert it into a fabrication  plan which  then you can follow and have a robot  walking around in front of you in about  30 minutes right so these are the sorts  of designs that  we know we can create  with a little bit of help from a human  in this case who is interacting with our  software  we're of course not the only people who  have done this there's a lot of uh  approaches that people take towards this  problem so what you're seeing on the  bottom left here is another system and  that was developed by stelian koroz's  group back when he was at cmu and they  were interested in well how do we also  incorporate the electromechanical  components the sensors the motors and  make sure that they have a place on the  robot body what you're seeing in the  middle  is um additional work where  encore meadow was thinking about well  how do we also take into account the  rigidity of the parts that need to be  created and how the fabrication  strategy  affects  how rigid those parts are  and so  different systems  over  time have been trying to solve this  problem of  computational robot design and have come  up with different solutions for  different aspects of the problem  so we've gotten a lot of functionality  out of these different systems  but one thing that i'd want to note is  that if we go back in time a little more  unfortunately the way that we solve  these problems and the ways that we  think about these problems compared to  approaches that showed up around 20  years ago  are not really fundamentally different  in that  back in 2000 there was also this idea of  well let's describe a robot  as a as  a graph made of rigid components and  joints and the joints of a controller  and the task is to move forward on flat  ground and we can evolve this robot and  we can also fabricate it right and so  these approaches that we've been  using in order to  make more interesting robotic structures  they allow us to  think about different types of tasks but  fundamentally the representations that  we're using for the robot and for the  task have not changed  at the same time when i show these  videos to people  they always look to me and they say well  those robots look like toys they're not  actually going to do anything right  and so these assumptions that we've made  in order to build these sorts of systems  give us  designs that move  they can move forward on flat planes  right sometimes they can move forward on  more complicated environments  but  the resulting physical designs that you  see